# Settings' storage page reads zram usage from /sys/block/zram0 -> (symlink)
# /sys/devices/virtual/block/zram0, which AOSP labels sysfs_zram via
# system/sepolicy/private/genfs_contexts. Both system_app and sysfs_zram are
# core types and the label is not a board fact, so this belongs in plat_private
# rather than in a BOARD_VENDOR_SEPOLICY_DIRS entry.
r_dir_file(system_app, sysfs_zram)

# Settings' memory page reads /proc/pagetypeinfo for its fragmentation readout.
# ProcessStats.java:621-624 opens it by name ("Parse /proc/pagetypeinfo and
# store the values") and :939 renders it, and that class runs in whichever
# process builds the stats -- Settings included, which is system_app.
#
# Nothing forbids the grant. The neverallow at
# system/sepolicy/private/app_neverallows.te:177-192 covers all_untrusted_apps,
# and that macro is an explicit set (:5-13: ephemeral_app, isolated_app,
# mediaprovider, untrusted_app, untrusted_app_25, untrusted_app_27,
# untrusted_app_all). system_app is not in it. AOSP already makes the identical
# grant to a core domain at private/system_server.te:852-857, and to incidentd
# and dumpstate.
#
# plat_private for the same reason as the sysfs_zram rule above: both operands
# are core types and neither label is a board fact.
#
# This was carried on tools/expected-avc-tuples.txt's REMOVED list for two
# sessions with a note saying exactly where the rule belonged and that it was
# "deliberately NOT added here: the sepolicy directory is owned by another
# agent". Nobody owned it. Settings lost the readout on every boot and the
# verifier called that normal.
allow system_app proc_pagetypeinfo:file r_file_perms;
